The cleanest way to share services/datastore/operations across all Wintersys ADT repositories without duplicating code is to extract it into a dedicated Git repository and pull it in as a Git submodule.
Because your toolkit uses #!/bin/sh, full binary paths, and dynamically resolves BUILD_HOME from /home/buildhome.dat, these scripts are already self-contained and portable across server roles (Build Machine, Web Server, Database, Reverse Proxy, Autoscaler, and Authenticator).

Recommended Approach: Dedicated Shared Repository + Git Submodule

Extract the shared datastore scripts into a standalone repository (for example, adt-datastore-services) and mount it at services/datastore/operations in each parent repository.

Why Git Submodules Fit Your Architecture

• Zero Runtime Overhead: Once cloned, files sit directly on disk at ${BUILD_HOME}/services/datastore/operations/ just like regular files—no symlinks, package managers, or extra build steps required.
• Version Pinning & Safe Rollouts: Each repository (e.g., adt-build-machine, adt-webserver, adt-database) pins a specific commit hash of the shared repository, preventing a breaking change in one tier from unexpectedly breaking another.
• Single Source of Truth: Bug fixes and new cloud provider support are committed once in adt-datastore-services and pulled into every repository on demand.

Step-by-Step Setup


1. Create the Shared Repository (adt-datastore-services)

Move the common scripts into their own repository with clean, self-contained layout:
text
adt-datastore-services/
├── README.md
├── MountDatastore.sh
├── ListDatastore.sh
├── ListFromDatastore.sh
├── GetFromDatastore.sh
├── PutToDatastore.sh
├── DeleteFromDatastore.sh
└── DeleteDatastore.sh
Use code with caution.

2. Attach as a Submodule in Each Consumer Repository

In adt-build-machine, adt-webserver, adt-database, etc.:
sh
git submodule add <git-repo-url>/adt-datastore-services.git services/datastore/operations
git commit -m "Add shared datastore operations submodule"
Use code with caution.

3. Update Your Bootstrap / Clone Commands

Ensure provisioning scripts that clone your repositories include --recurse-submodules:
sh
git clone --recurse-submodules <git-repo-url>/adt-build-machine.git
Use code with caution.
(Or run git submodule update --init --recursive immediately after cloning an existing checkout.)

Alternative Options & Trade-Offs


Approach	Best When	Key Trade-Offs
Git Submodule (Recommended)	You want version-pinned, zero-dependency files directly at services/datastore/operations across GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket.	Requires --recurse-submodules during git clone and an extra git submodule update --remote step when pulling updates.
Git Subtree	You want git clone to work without --recurse-submodules while still syncing from an upstream shared repo.	Commit history becomes noisier when merging upstream updates back and forth.
Central System Install (/opt/wintersys-adt/common)	All server tiers are provisioned by a single base image or bootstrap script that installs shared libraries system-wide.	Requires updating every script path or maintaining symlinks inside ${BUILD_HOME}/services/datastore/operations.


One Architectural Detail to Check

Before sharing the scripts across all repositories, verify that every helper script they call (LogStatusMessages.sh, GetVariableValue.sh) exists at the exact same relative path under ${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/services/ on both the Build Machine and target server tiers (Web Server, Database, etc.), or include those core helpers in the same shared submodule.
If you'd like help setting this up, let me know:
• Whether your target server repos (webserver, database, etc.) use the exact same ${BUILD_HOME}/helpers/services/ layout as the Build Machine
• If you want the exact Git commands to split services/datastore/operations out of your existing repo while preserving its commit history
